---
title: Grammar of Graphics
description: Understand TanStack Charts as a composition of data, marks, channels, scales, guides, and layers.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts follows the grammar-of-graphics tradition established by\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://doi.org/10.1007/0-387-28695-0","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Leland Wilkinson"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and developed through\nprojects such as "},{"type":"link","href":"https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ggplot2"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://vega.github.io/vega-lite/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vega-Lite"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and Observable Plot. Observable\nPlot is the closest API influence for mark-local data, channels, and layered\ncomposition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grammar describes "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"what visual encodings mean"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and lets the runtime decide\nhow to lay them out and render them. A chart is not a special-purpose component\nwith a fixed series model. It is a composition of:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — the observations or derived rows a mark consumes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — geometric forms such as lines, bars, dots, areas, rules, or text."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — mappings from data to position, grouping, color, radius, or identity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — callable factories or instances that map semantic values into visual coordinates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guides"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — axes, ticks, grids, titles, and legends that explain those mappings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — marks rendered together in declaration order."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The result is one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpec"},{"type":"text","value":" compiled into a renderer-neutral scene."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-smallest-useful-declaration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The smallest useful declaration"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\ninterface LetterFrequency {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nconst alphabet: readonly LetterFrequency[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [barY(alphabet, { x: 'letter', y: 'frequency' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleBand },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark consumes the typed letter-frequency rows directly and maps their\nexisting fields to x and y. No universal series wrapper or renamed chart\nfields sit between the source data and the mark."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lightweight scale package covers these common numeric and categorical\nmappings. "},{"type":"link","href":"./scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" explains when a chart needs D3 instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"data-belongs-to-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data belongs to marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each mark receives its own iterable:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const marks = [\n  areaY(forecastRows, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y1: 'low',\n    y2: 'high',\n  }),\n  lineY(actualRows, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y: 'value',\n  }),\n  ruleY([target]),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The arrays may have different lengths and datum types. There is no required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ series: [...] }"},{"type":"text","value":" wrapper and no requirement to reshape unrelated layers into one table. This keeps simple charts simple and lets custom compositions use the data model that naturally represents each layer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a transform creates new rows, run that transform before the mark. Memoize expensive derived rows through application or framework reactivity. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./chart-definitions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart Definitions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"marks-choose-geometry","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks choose geometry"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A mark turns data and channel values into scene nodes and interaction points:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"lineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  z: 'region',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineY"},{"type":"text","value":" chooses connected line geometry."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" map compatible fields to positional channels."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" partitions observations into independent lines and feeds the default categorical color mapping."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in marks infer observation identity from a unique top-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", nested\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.id"},{"type":"text","value":", or mark-specific positional value. Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" only when none\nrepresents the entity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a mark for the analytical task, then layer other marks to add context. "},{"type":"link","href":"./marks-and-layering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks and Layering"}]},{"type":"text","value":" describes the built-in families."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"channels-map-data-to-meaning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Channels map data to meaning"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A channel is usually a compatible field name:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"dot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  z: 'segment',\n  r: 'accounts',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It can also be an accessor when the value is derived:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"dot(rows, {\n  x: (row) => row.revenue / row.accounts,\n  y: 'retention',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessors receive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(datum, { index, data })"},{"type":"text","value":" and remain fully typed. Field\nchannels are filtered by the value type the mark accepts, so TypeScript rejects\na date field where a numeric bar length is required."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Channels describe mappings. Constant appearance options such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke: '#2563eb'"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fillOpacity: 0.2"},{"type":"text","value":" describe a fixed style. The distinction keeps semantic encodings visible in source."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read "},{"type":"link","href":"./data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for missing values, accessors, keys, grouping, color, and radius."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scale-factories-derive-semantic-space","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scale factories derive semantic space"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a factory when its domain should follow the mark channels:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n\nconst axes = {\n  x: {\n    scale: scalePoint,\n    axis: { label: 'Month' },\n  },\n  y: {\n    scale: scaleLinear,\n    nice: true,\n    grid: true,\n    axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The marks supply the domain, the factory supplies the mapping, and TanStack\nCharts supplies the responsive range. Pass a configured scale instance when\nthe application owns a fixed domain."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"guides-explain-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guides explain scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Axis guide options live next to their scale:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const y = {\n  scale: revenueScale,\n  grid: true,\n  axis: {\n    label: 'Monthly revenue',\n    ticks: {\n      count: 5,\n      format: (value: number) => `${Math.round(value / 1_000)}k`,\n    },\n  },\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The scale maps values. The guide makes that mapping legible. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ticks"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"format"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reverse"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tickRotate"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"labelOffset"},{"type":"text","value":" are presentation controls for the axis; they do not replace scale semantics."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Omitted margins are measured from the actual guides. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./layout-axes-and-coordinates.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layout, Axes, and Coordinates"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"layers-build-richer-charts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layers build richer charts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Marks render in array order. Put context behind the primary data and annotations above it:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"layered-chart-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"layered-chart","data-collapsed-indexes":"[]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { areaY, defineChart, dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\n\\nconst rows = [\\n  { month: 'Jan', value: 14 },\\n  { month: 'Feb', value: 18 },\\n  { month: 'Mar', value: 16 },\\n  { month: 'Apr', value: 23 },\\n  { month: 'May', value: 27 },\\n  { month: 'Jun', value: 25 },\\n]\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    areaY(rows, {\\n      x: 'month',\\n      y: 'value',\\n      fill: '#93c5fd',\\n      fillOpacity: 0.35,\\n    }),\\n    lineY(rows, {\\n      x: 'month',\\n      y: 'value',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 2,\\n    }),\\n    dot(rows, {\\n      x: 'month',\\n      y: 'value',\\n      r: 4,\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: {\\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12),\\n    },\\n    y: {\\n      scale: scaleLinear,\\n      nice: true,\\n      grid: true,\\n      axis: { label: 'Value' },\\n    },\\n  },\\n})\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { areaY, defineChart, dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', value: 14 },\n  { month: 'Feb', value: 18 },\n  { month: 'Mar', value: 16 },\n  { month: 'Apr', value: 23 },\n  { month: 'May', value: 27 },\n  { month: 'Jun', value: 25 },\n]\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      fill: '#93c5fd',\n      fillOpacity: 0.35,\n    }),\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 2,\n    }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      r: 4,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12),\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Value' },\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=layered-chart env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The area establishes context, the line carries the trend, and the dots keep\neach observation visible. All three marks share the same rows and scales."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"definitions-compile-the-grammar","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definitions compile the grammar"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineChart"},{"type":"text","value":" preserves the relationship between datum types, channel values, configured scales, axes, scenes, and interaction callbacks."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"object definition"}]},{"type":"text","value":" closes over stable data and options."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"responsive definition"}]},{"type":"text","value":" receives the current size and default build-time\ntheme."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The definition is also the application memoization boundary. Keep reusable\ndefinitions at module scope. In a component, memoize the complete definition\nagainst the application values it captures."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rendering-is-downstream","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rendering is downstream"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grammar does not contain DOM or framework lifecycle code. It compiles to a keyed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScene"},{"type":"text","value":" containing:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved chart and margin bounds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved x, y, and color mappings"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Renderer-neutral scene nodes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction points that retain original data"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Theme and gradient resources"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The built-in SVG renderer, DOM and framework hosts, static exporter, and custom\nrenderers all consume that same result."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Continue with "},{"type":"link","href":"./chart-definitions.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart Definitions"}]},{"type":"text","value":", or start from a task in "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/choosing-a-chart.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choosing a Chart"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-smallest-useful-declaration","text":"The smallest useful declaration","level":2},{"id":"data-belongs-to-marks","text":"Data belongs to marks","level":2},{"id":"marks-choose-geometry","text":"Marks choose geometry","level":2},{"id":"channels-map-data-to-meaning","text":"Channels map data to meaning","level":2},{"id":"scale-factories-derive-semantic-space","text":"Scale factories derive semantic space","level":2},{"id":"guides-explain-scales","text":"Guides explain scales","level":2},{"id":"layers-build-richer-charts","text":"Layers build richer charts","level":2},{"id":"definitions-compile-the-grammar","text":"Definitions compile the grammar","level":2},{"id":"rendering-is-downstream","text":"Rendering is downstream","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts follows the grammar-of-graphics tradition established by\n[Leland Wilkinson](https://doi.org/10.1007/0-387-28695-0) and developed through\nprojects such as [ggplot2](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/),\n[Vega-Lite](https://vega.github.io/vega-lite/), and Observable Plot. Observable\nPlot is the closest API influence for mark-local data, channels, and layered\ncomposition.\n\nThe grammar describes **what visual encodings mean** and lets the runtime decide\nhow to lay them out and render them. A chart is not a special-purpose component\nwith a fixed series model. It is a composition of:\n\n1. **Data** — the observations or derived rows a mark consumes.\n2. **Marks** — geometric forms such as lines, bars, dots, areas, rules, or text.\n3. **Channels** — mappings from data to position, grouping, color, radius, or identity.\n4. **Scales** — callable factories or instances that map semantic values into visual coordinates.\n5. **Guides** — axes, ticks, grids, titles, and legends that explain those mappings.\n6. **Layers** — marks rendered together in declaration order.\n\nThe result is one `ChartSpec` compiled into a renderer-neutral scene.\n\n## The smallest useful declaration\n\n```ts\nimport { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\ninterface LetterFrequency {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nconst alphabet: readonly LetterFrequency[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [barY(alphabet, { x: 'letter', y: 'frequency' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleBand },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe mark consumes the typed letter-frequency rows directly and maps their\nexisting fields to x and y. No universal series wrapper or renamed chart\nfields sit between the source data and the mark.\n\nThe lightweight scale package covers these common numeric and categorical\nmappings. [Scales](./scales-and-d3.md) explains when a chart needs D3 instead.\n\n## Data belongs to marks\n\nEach mark receives its own iterable:\n\n```ts\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(forecastRows, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y1: 'low',\n    y2: 'high',\n  }),\n  lineY(actualRows, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y: 'value',\n  }),\n  ruleY([target]),\n]\n```\n\nThe arrays may have different lengths and datum types. There is no required `{ series: [...] }` wrapper and no requirement to reshape unrelated layers into one table. This keeps simple charts simple and lets custom compositions use the data model that naturally represents each layer.\n\nIf a transform creates new rows, run that transform before the mark. Memoize expensive derived rows through application or framework reactivity. See [Chart Definitions](./chart-definitions.md).\n\n## Marks choose geometry\n\nA mark turns data and channel values into scene nodes and interaction points:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\n- `lineY` chooses connected line geometry.\n- `x` and `y` map compatible fields to positional channels.\n- `z` partitions observations into independent lines and feeds the default categorical color mapping.\n\nBuilt-in marks infer observation identity from a unique top-level `id`, nested\n`data.id`, or mark-specific positional value. Add `key` only when none\nrepresents the entity.\n\nChoose a mark for the analytical task, then layer other marks to add context. [Marks and Layering](./marks-and-layering.md) describes the built-in families.\n\n## Channels map data to meaning\n\nA channel is usually a compatible field name:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  z: 'segment',\n  r: 'accounts',\n})\n```\n\nIt can also be an accessor when the value is derived:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: (row) => row.revenue / row.accounts,\n  y: 'retention',\n})\n```\n\nAccessors receive `(datum, { index, data })` and remain fully typed. Field\nchannels are filtered by the value type the mark accepts, so TypeScript rejects\na date field where a numeric bar length is required.\n\nChannels describe mappings. Constant appearance options such as `stroke: '#2563eb'` or `fillOpacity: 0.2` describe a fixed style. The distinction keeps semantic encodings visible in source.\n\nRead [Data and Channels](./data-and-channels.md) for missing values, accessors, keys, grouping, color, and radius.\n\n## Scale factories derive semantic space\n\nPass a factory when its domain should follow the mark channels:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n\nconst axes = {\n  x: {\n    scale: scalePoint,\n    axis: { label: 'Month' },\n  },\n  y: {\n    scale: scaleLinear,\n    nice: true,\n    grid: true,\n    axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThe marks supply the domain, the factory supplies the mapping, and TanStack\nCharts supplies the responsive range. Pass a configured scale instance when\nthe application owns a fixed domain.\n\n## Guides explain scales\n\nAxis guide options live next to their scale:\n\n```ts\nconst y = {\n  scale: revenueScale,\n  grid: true,\n  axis: {\n    label: 'Monthly revenue',\n    ticks: {\n      count: 5,\n      format: (value: number) => `${Math.round(value / 1_000)}k`,\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThe scale maps values. The guide makes that mapping legible. `ticks`, `format`, `label`, `grid`, `reverse`, `tickRotate`, and `labelOffset` are presentation controls for the axis; they do not replace scale semantics.\n\nOmitted margins are measured from the actual guides. See [Layout, Axes, and Coordinates](./layout-axes-and-coordinates.md).\n\n## Layers build richer charts\n\nMarks render in array order. Put context behind the primary data and annotations above it:\n\n```ts group=layered-chart env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { areaY, defineChart, dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', value: 14 },\n  { month: 'Feb', value: 18 },\n  { month: 'Mar', value: 16 },\n  { month: 'Apr', value: 23 },\n  { month: 'May', value: 27 },\n  { month: 'Jun', value: 25 },\n]\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      fill: '#93c5fd',\n      fillOpacity: 0.35,\n    }),\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 2,\n    }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      r: 4,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12),\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Value' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe area establishes context, the line carries the trend, and the dots keep\neach observation visible. All three marks share the same rows and scales.\n\n## Definitions compile the grammar\n\n`defineChart` preserves the relationship between datum types, channel values, configured scales, axes, scenes, and interaction callbacks.\n\n- An **object definition** closes over stable data and options.\n- A **responsive definition** receives the current size and default build-time\n  theme.\n\nThe definition is also the application memoization boundary. Keep reusable\ndefinitions at module scope. In a component, memoize the complete definition\nagainst the application values it captures.\n\n## Rendering is downstream\n\nThe grammar does not contain DOM or framework lifecycle code. It compiles to a keyed `ChartScene` containing:\n\n- Resolved chart and margin bounds\n- Resolved x, y, and color mappings\n- Renderer-neutral scene nodes\n- Interaction points that retain original data\n- Theme and gradient resources\n\nThe built-in SVG renderer, DOM and framework hosts, static exporter, and custom\nrenderers all consume that same result.\n\nContinue with [Chart Definitions](./chart-definitions.md), or start from a task in [Choosing a Chart](../guides/choosing-a-chart.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Grammar of Graphics\ndescription: Understand TanStack Charts as a composition of data, marks, channels, scales, guides, and layers.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts follows the grammar-of-graphics tradition established by\n[Leland Wilkinson](https://doi.org/10.1007/0-387-28695-0) and developed through\nprojects such as [ggplot2](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/),\n[Vega-Lite](https://vega.github.io/vega-lite/), and Observable Plot. Observable\nPlot is the closest API influence for mark-local data, channels, and layered\ncomposition.\n\nThe grammar describes **what visual encodings mean** and lets the runtime decide\nhow to lay them out and render them. A chart is not a special-purpose component\nwith a fixed series model. It is a composition of:\n\n1. **Data** — the observations or derived rows a mark consumes.\n2. **Marks** — geometric forms such as lines, bars, dots, areas, rules, or text.\n3. **Channels** — mappings from data to position, grouping, color, radius, or identity.\n4. **Scales** — callable factories or instances that map semantic values into visual coordinates.\n5. **Guides** — axes, ticks, grids, titles, and legends that explain those mappings.\n6. **Layers** — marks rendered together in declaration order.\n\nThe result is one `ChartSpec` compiled into a renderer-neutral scene.\n\n## The smallest useful declaration\n\n```ts\nimport { barY, defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\ninterface LetterFrequency {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nconst alphabet: readonly LetterFrequency[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n]\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [barY(alphabet, { x: 'letter', y: 'frequency' })],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleBand },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, nice: true },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe mark consumes the typed letter-frequency rows directly and maps their\nexisting fields to x and y. No universal series wrapper or renamed chart\nfields sit between the source data and the mark.\n\nThe lightweight scale package covers these common numeric and categorical\nmappings. [Scales](./scales-and-d3.md) explains when a chart needs D3 instead.\n\n## Data belongs to marks\n\nEach mark receives its own iterable:\n\n```ts\nconst marks = [\n  areaY(forecastRows, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y1: 'low',\n    y2: 'high',\n  }),\n  lineY(actualRows, {\n    x: 'date',\n    y: 'value',\n  }),\n  ruleY([target]),\n]\n```\n\nThe arrays may have different lengths and datum types. There is no required `{ series: [...] }` wrapper and no requirement to reshape unrelated layers into one table. This keeps simple charts simple and lets custom compositions use the data model that naturally represents each layer.\n\nIf a transform creates new rows, run that transform before the mark. Memoize expensive derived rows through application or framework reactivity. See [Chart Definitions](./chart-definitions.md).\n\n## Marks choose geometry\n\nA mark turns data and channel values into scene nodes and interaction points:\n\n```ts\nlineY(rows, {\n  x: 'date',\n  y: 'revenue',\n  z: 'region',\n})\n```\n\n- `lineY` chooses connected line geometry.\n- `x` and `y` map compatible fields to positional channels.\n- `z` partitions observations into independent lines and feeds the default categorical color mapping.\n\nBuilt-in marks infer observation identity from a unique top-level `id`, nested\n`data.id`, or mark-specific positional value. Add `key` only when none\nrepresents the entity.\n\nChoose a mark for the analytical task, then layer other marks to add context. [Marks and Layering](./marks-and-layering.md) describes the built-in families.\n\n## Channels map data to meaning\n\nA channel is usually a compatible field name:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'revenue',\n  y: 'retention',\n  z: 'segment',\n  r: 'accounts',\n})\n```\n\nIt can also be an accessor when the value is derived:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: (row) => row.revenue / row.accounts,\n  y: 'retention',\n})\n```\n\nAccessors receive `(datum, { index, data })` and remain fully typed. Field\nchannels are filtered by the value type the mark accepts, so TypeScript rejects\na date field where a numeric bar length is required.\n\nChannels describe mappings. Constant appearance options such as `stroke: '#2563eb'` or `fillOpacity: 0.2` describe a fixed style. The distinction keeps semantic encodings visible in source.\n\nRead [Data and Channels](./data-and-channels.md) for missing values, accessors, keys, grouping, color, and radius.\n\n## Scale factories derive semantic space\n\nPass a factory when its domain should follow the mark channels:\n\n```ts\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\n\nconst axes = {\n  x: {\n    scale: scalePoint,\n    axis: { label: 'Month' },\n  },\n  y: {\n    scale: scaleLinear,\n    nice: true,\n    grid: true,\n    axis: { label: 'Revenue' },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThe marks supply the domain, the factory supplies the mapping, and TanStack\nCharts supplies the responsive range. Pass a configured scale instance when\nthe application owns a fixed domain.\n\n## Guides explain scales\n\nAxis guide options live next to their scale:\n\n```ts\nconst y = {\n  scale: revenueScale,\n  grid: true,\n  axis: {\n    label: 'Monthly revenue',\n    ticks: {\n      count: 5,\n      format: (value: number) => `${Math.round(value / 1_000)}k`,\n    },\n  },\n}\n```\n\nThe scale maps values. The guide makes that mapping legible. `ticks`, `format`, `label`, `grid`, `reverse`, `tickRotate`, and `labelOffset` are presentation controls for the axis; they do not replace scale semantics.\n\nOmitted margins are measured from the actual guides. See [Layout, Axes, and Coordinates](./layout-axes-and-coordinates.md).\n\n## Layers build richer charts\n\nMarks render in array order. Put context behind the primary data and annotations above it:\n\n```ts group=layered-chart env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { areaY, defineChart, dot, lineY } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst rows = [\n  { month: 'Jan', value: 14 },\n  { month: 'Feb', value: 18 },\n  { month: 'Mar', value: 16 },\n  { month: 'Apr', value: 23 },\n  { month: 'May', value: 27 },\n  { month: 'Jun', value: 25 },\n]\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    areaY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      fill: '#93c5fd',\n      fillOpacity: 0.35,\n    }),\n    lineY(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 2,\n    }),\n    dot(rows, {\n      x: 'month',\n      y: 'value',\n      r: 4,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: {\n      scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12),\n    },\n    y: {\n      scale: scaleLinear,\n      nice: true,\n      grid: true,\n      axis: { label: 'Value' },\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe area establishes context, the line carries the trend, and the dots keep\neach observation visible. All three marks share the same rows and scales.\n\n## Definitions compile the grammar\n\n`defineChart` preserves the relationship between datum types, channel values, configured scales, axes, scenes, and interaction callbacks.\n\n- An **object definition** closes over stable data and options.\n- A **responsive definition** receives the current size and default build-time\n  theme.\n\nThe definition is also the application memoization boundary. Keep reusable\ndefinitions at module scope. In a component, memoize the complete definition\nagainst the application values it captures.\n\n## Rendering is downstream\n\nThe grammar does not contain DOM or framework lifecycle code. It compiles to a keyed `ChartScene` containing:\n\n- Resolved chart and margin bounds\n- Resolved x, y, and color mappings\n- Renderer-neutral scene nodes\n- Interaction points that retain original data\n- Theme and gradient resources\n\nThe built-in SVG renderer, DOM and framework hosts, static exporter, and custom\nrenderers all consume that same result.\n\nContinue with [Chart Definitions](./chart-definitions.md), or start from a task in [Choosing a Chart](../guides/choosing-a-chart.md).\n"}
